    "run_id": "V2L4",
    "ui_node_id": "Topology/Lin20K3/main-theorem#thm: main",
    "state": {
      "workspace": "./examples/demo",
      "session": "state-20260924-V2L4",
      "current_node": {
        "id": "Topology/Lin20K3/main/thm: main",
        "title": "thm: main",
        "description": "The Dehn twist along the separating neck sphere in $K3\\#K3$ is not smoothly isotopic to the identity even after one connected sum with $S^2\\times S^2$.\n\nBoth spin structures of the stabilized mapping torus have nonzero $G$-equivariant invariant: the product formula makes the characteristic degree vanish, Euler-class vanishing would force restriction to vanish, and Kronheimer--Mrowka compute the nonequivariant class as $\\eta^3\\ne0$. This contradicts product-family vanishing.\n\n## Proof strategy\n\nThe mapping torus of the neck twist is the spin connected sum of a product K3 family and a twisted K3 family. Its two spin structures become the two product/twisted arrangements after stabilization. For either arrangement write the unstabilized invariant as $\\alpha$. The connected-sum product has characteristic degree zero by the product-vanishing lemma; the stabilized invariant is $\\alpha e_{\\widetilde{\\mathbb R}}$. If it vanished, the key proposition would force its $S^1$ restriction, and hence its nonequivariant restriction, to vanish. Kronheimer--Mrowka Proposition 5.1 gives the latter as $\\eta^3\\ne0\\in\\pi_3^S$, with the two definitions identified by the framed-cobordism lemma. Thus both ",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "project": "Topology/Lin20K3",
        "source_locations": [
          {
            "end_line": 36,
            "file": "blueprint/Topology/Lin20K3/main-theorem.md",
            "kind": "theorem",
            "start_line": 12
          },
